																	<a href="/forum/t-13930855/incorporeal-mass-at-the-center-of-the-earth#post-4859184">Re: Incorporeal mass at the center of the Earth</a>
									</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/undercover-fly" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7107990); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=7107990&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609488015" alt="Undercover Fly" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=7107990)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/undercover-fly" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7107990); return false;" >Undercover Fly</a></span> <span class="odate time_1609486917 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 Jan 2021 07:41</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-89000/help:ideas-critique">Writing Help / Help: Ideas Critique</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13930855/incorporeal-mass-at-the-center-of-the-earth">Incorporeal mass at the center of the Earth</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4859184">
									

<blockquote>
<p><em>How does the computer communicate from the center of the earth? I honestly can't imagine just handwaving this sorta thing… how does a signal travel from an incorporeal electronic device to physical electronic devices, through miles and miles of solid mass?</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p>It's a Foundation long-distance communicator utilizing anomalous tech. Given that the Foundation has communication methods that can communicate extra-dimensionally and that most types of communication are intangible anyway, I don't feel communicating with an intangible computer is much of a stretch. And Foundation technology pretty much renders distance and matter separations irrelevant.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Why are there so many bodies down there?</strong> Like what is happening to make this occur? I can't imagine people are being turned incorporeal this often… <strong>If they are, why isn't that the SCP and not the bodies at the center of the earth?</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p>The anomaly is the collection of everyone who has ever become intangible and fallen through the floor and it has been building up over many thousands of years. The bodies come from as far-flung sources as ancient trickster gods, experimental para-technology from an Occult war and quite a few from previous iterations of <a href="/scp-2000">SCP-2000</a>. Lastly while most bodies are dead, none have decomposed due to their intangible nature. As for some central object or entity causing this, that's simply not the case. This isn't a sinister plot or experiment gone wrong, this is an inevitability in a world with intangibility.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>If the agents are deaf, dumb and blind (<em>I don't know why because the post doesn't say, but I assume because of their status deep in the earth</em>)… how can they tell the Foundation anything? They have no sensation (<em>because tactile is out, due to their incorporeality</em>).</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>I still think there isn't much to this narrative. It feels like a description of people suffering… but what is the arc here? Just &quot;we're intangible, and at the center of the earth!&quot; and then dying? (<em>Also how would the agents even know about that they're in the center of the earth?</em>)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Good point. I have changed the original post to explain how the agents understand and can interact with their situation.</p>
<p>Which is that they <strong>don't</strong>.</p>
<p>To the agents, they have been plunged into complete darkness, unable to move or touch anything, with a faint sensation of things (the other intangible people) filling the same space as them. To the agent with the computer, the computer is an outlet through by which they can call for help and via which may one day be rescued. So they desperately type at the computer, relying solely on muscle memory, not knowing whether what they are typing is being revived and unable to know if anyone has replied. The narrative is the story of that agents' experience, how they go from fearing, to hopeful, to desperate, and the implication that many other people have gone through that experience without the chance of salvation and will continue doing so indefinitely. It's a description of people suffering, but the story of one of those people shows how hopeless, prolonged and empty that suffering is.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
<em>As a courtesy to our readers on mobile devices, please collapse long posts. ~Zyn</em>
